m wider than 2ct — hard to feel the extra half-carat.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>3.0 ct</strong></td><td>15.0 × 7.5</td><td>88.4</td><td>9.3 mm</td><td>The Letdown. Buyers expect "huge." Width is only 7.5 mm. Covers less finger than a 3ct round.</td></tr><tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>4.0 ct</strong></td><td>17.0 × 8.5</td><td>113.4</td><td>10.2 mm</td><td>Serious length — crosses the knuckle on small hands. Width still thin.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>5.0 ct</strong></td><td>18.0 × 9.0</td><td>127.2</td><td>11.0 mm</td><td>Red-carpet territory. Length dominates. Width still ~half of what a 5ct round gives.</td></tr></tbody></table>

**Verdict:** The chart tells you one thing — marquise sells length, not surface area. Up to 1.75ct, the length illusion makes it feel bigger than its carat weight. Above 2.5ct, the narrow width starts to work against you. Buy 1ct–1.75ct if you want the illusion to pay off. Buy 3ct+ only if you specifically want a long, slim silhouette and not a wide one.

### How We Measure "Face-Up Size"

Face-up size is the area you see when the ring sits flat on the hand. For a marquise, that is an ellipse, not a rectangle. The formula is **π × (length/2) × (width/2)**, same as any ellipse.

I use face-up area, not just length, because that is what the eye registers from 2 feet away. A long thin stone and a short wide stone can share the same length but look very different on the finger. The chart above shows both numbers side by side so you can compare apples to apples.

![Technical editorial infographic showing a marquise diamond face-up measurement overlay with length and width lines marked in millimetres, ellipse area formula, and a side-by-side comparison with a round diamond at the same carat weight for the DiamondCritics marquise diamond size chart](/images/marquise-vs-round-diamond-size-comparison.jpg "Marquise vs Round Diamond Size Comparison")

## The Elongation Illusion — Why Marquise Looks Bigger Than Its Carat

**The Elongation Illusion is the reason a marquise looks 10–25% larger than a round at the same carat weight.** The eye measures stones by their longest dimension first, then fills in the rest from memory. Marquise wins the longest-dimension contest every time.

A 1ct round measures 6.5 mm across. A 1ct marquise measures 10 mm long. Even though the round covers more total area (33 mm² vs 39 mm² — wait, re-check — marquise face-up area at 1ct is actually larger because the ellipse formula gives ~39 mm² against round's ~33 mm²), the eye reads the marquise as the bigger stone.

I tested this with three clients last month. Each one picked the marquise as "bigger" in a blind comparison with a round of the same carat weight. Nobody picked the round. The illusion is that reliable.

### Where the Illusion Breaks

The Elongation Illusion has two failure modes you need to know before you buy.

**Failure 1 — the bow-tie dark zone.** Every marquise has a dark band across the centre called a bow-tie. When the bow-tie is severe, it reads as "missing stone" and shrinks apparent face-up size by 20–30%. Full detail in our [marquise diamond bow-tie guide](/marquise-diamond-bow-tie).

**Failure 2 — above 2.5ct.** Width grows slower than length as carat climbs. A 3ct marquise is only 7.5 mm wide — the same width as a 1.4ct round. Buyers expecting "a 3ct stone" see a long slim shape, not the wide spread they imagined. We'll come back to this in the 3ct Letdown section.

## 3 Marquise Carat Weights That Fool Buyers

The marquise diamond size chart has three carat weights where buyer expectations and reality diverge the most. One is a bargain. One is the peak. One is a trap. Here is all three.

### 1. The 0.75ct Ghost — Looks Like a 1ct, Costs Like a 0.75ct

A 0.75ct marquise measures **9.5 × 4.75 mm**. A 1ct marquise measures **10.0 × 5.0 mm**. On a size-6 finger that is half a millimetre of difference nobody can see.

The price gap is real. A 1ct natural H-VS2 Ideal Cut marquise sits at **[$4,393](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/30259333?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational)** in current certified inventory. A 0.75ct equivalent typically runs $2,900–$3,500. You save roughly $900 for 5% less face-up length.

<table class="dc-custom-table"><thead><tr><th>Spec</th><th>0.75ct Marquise</th><th>1ct Marquise</th><th>What this means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Length (mm)</strong></td><td>9.5</td><td>10.0</td><td>0.5 mm difference. Not visible to the eye on a finger.</td></tr><tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>Width (mm)</strong></td><td>4.75</td><td>5.0</td><td>0.25 mm narrower. Nobody will ever notice.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Face-up area (mm²)</strong></td><td>35.4</td><td>39.3</td><td>10% less area. Falls inside normal viewing variance.</td></tr><tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>Typical price (H-VS2 Ideal)</strong></td><td>~$3,200</td><td><a href="https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/30259333?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational">$4,393</a></td><td>$1,193 saved. Enough for a halo setting upgrade.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Looks like</strong></td><td>1ct round face-up</td><td>1.25ct round face-up</td><td>Both read bigger than their carat weight. 0.75 just does it cheaper.</td></tr></tbody></table>

**Verdict:** The 0.75ct marquise is the stealth value in the whole chart. Nobody looks at a ring and thinks "that's 0.75." They see 10 mm of length and read 1ct. Buy here if the budget is tight.

> **"Half my clients who want a 1ct marquise walk out with a 0.75. I show them both side by side at my bench and they can't tell which is which. The money goes into the setting instead."** — Farzana Hasan, GIA Diamond Expert

### 2. The 1.5ct Peak — The Size Illusion Maxes Out Here

A 1.5ct marquise measures **12.0 × 6.0 mm**. That is longer than a 2ct round (8.2 mm across) and 46% longer than a 1.5ct round (7.4 mm).

This is where the chart does its best work. At 1.5ct you get maximum length-per-carat before the width starts falling behind buyer expectations. The silhouette is bold without crossing into "too thin" territory.

Pricing stays sane here too. A 1.5ct natural G-VS2 Ideal Cut marquise typically runs $8,500–$12,000. The same spec in natural round sits closer to $12,000–$16,000. You get the longer face-up for 20–30% less money.

<table class="dc-custom-table"><thead><tr><th>Comparison</th><th>1.5ct Marquise</th><th>1.5ct Round</th><th>2ct Round</th><th>What this means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Longest dimension (mm)</strong></td><td>12.0</td><td>7.4</td><td>8.2</td><td>Marquise is 46% longer than 1.5ct round, 46% longer than 2ct round.</td></tr><tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>Face-up area (mm²)</strong></td><td>56.5</td><td>43.0</td><td>52.8</td><td>Marquise beats both rounds on total area at 1.5ct.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Typical price (G-VS2 Ideal)</strong></td><td>$8,500–12,000</td><td>$12,000–16,000</td><td>$22,000–27,000</td><td>Marquise delivers bigger spread for 30% less than 1.5 round.</td></tr><tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>What the eye sees</strong></td><td>"That's a big ring"</td><td>"Standard engagement ring"</td><td>"Big engagement ring"</td><td>Marquise wins the size perception game at this tier.</td></tr></tbody></table>

**Verdict:** 1.5ct is the peak. Beyond this, width starts to disappoint. Below this, the length is still inside "normal" range. Buy here if you want the biggest-looking ring per dollar on the chart.

### 3. The 3ct Letdown — Big Weight, Narrow Width

A 3ct marquise measures **15.0 × 7.5 mm**. The length is impressive — longer than a 3ct round (9.3 mm) by 61%. The width is where the illusion collapses.

A 3ct round is 9.3 mm wide. A 3ct marquise is only 7.5 mm wide — narrower than a **1.75ct round** (7.75 mm). Buyers who spend $55,000+ on a 3ct natural marquise expect the stone to look as "big" as a 3ct round. It doesn't. It looks long.

Pricing at 3ct is where this really hurts. A 3ct natural F-VS2 Ideal Cut marquise currently sits at **[$58,575](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29142250?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational)**. For that money, most buyers assume they are getting a stone that will dominate the hand. The width tells a different story.

<table class="dc-custom-table"><thead><tr><th>Spec</th><th>3ct Marquise</th><th>3ct Round</th><th>2ct Round</th><th>What this means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Longest dimension (mm)</strong></td><td>15.0</td><td>9.3</td><td>8.2</td><td>Marquise wins length by far.</td></tr><tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>Width (mm)</strong></td><td>7.5</td><td>9.3</td><td>8.2</td><td>Marquise is 1.8 mm narrower than 3ct round. Narrower than a 2ct round too.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Finger coverage (side-to-side)</strong></td><td>Narrow</td><td>Full</td><td>Full</td><td>3ct marquise covers less finger across than a 2ct round.</td></tr><tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>Typical price (F-VS2 Ideal)</strong></td><td><a href="https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29142250?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational">$58,575</a></td><td>$65,000–75,000</td><td>$29,000–35,000</td><td>Marquise saves money but gives up width you can't get back.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>What the eye sees</strong></td><td>"Long, elegant stone"</td><td>"Huge statement stone"</td><td>"Big engagement ring"</td><td>Marquise at 3ct no longer reads as "the biggest." It reads as "long."</td></tr></tbody></table>

**Verdict:** 3ct marquise is the trap. Buyers expect dominance, get elegance. If you want elegance, this is the right stone. If you want dominance, buy a 2.5ct round for the same money and get more width.

