## Marquise vs Round Face-Up Size — Side by Side at Every Carat

The headline comparison most buyers want: does my marquise look bigger than my friend's round at the same carat? Short answer: yes, up to about 2ct. Beyond that, it depends on which measurement matters to you.

<table class="dc-custom-table"><thead><tr><th>Carat</th><th>Marquise L × W (mm)</th><th>Round Ø (mm)</th><th>Marquise Area / Round Area</th><th>What this means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>0.5 ct</strong></td><td>8.0 × 4.0</td><td>5.15</td><td>25.1 / 20.8 (+21%)</td><td>Marquise wins on both length and area.</td></tr><tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>0.75 ct</strong></td><td>9.5 × 4.75</td><td>5.9</td><td>35.4 / 27.3 (+30%)</td><td>Peak area advantage. The Ghost tier.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>1 ct</strong></td><td>10.0 × 5.0</td><td>6.5</td><td>39.3 / 33.2 (+18%)</td><td>Classic 1ct comparison. Marquise still wins by nearly a full carat perception.</td></tr><tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>1.5 ct</strong></td><td>12.0 × 6.0</td><td>7.4</td><td>56.5 / 43.0 (+31%)</td><td>The Peak. Biggest absolute spread advantage.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>2 ct</strong></td><td>13.5 × 6.75</td><td>8.2</td><td>71.6 / 52.8 (+36%)</td><td>Still winning area, but round is catching up on width.</td></tr><tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>2.5 ct</strong></td><td>14.5 × 7.25</td><td>8.8</td><td>82.5 / 60.8 (+36%)</td><td>Width parity getting close. Round width 8.8, marquise 7.25.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>3 ct</strong></td><td>15.0 × 7.5</td><td>9.3</td><td>88.4 / 67.9 (+30%)</td><td>Area still bigger but round now wider. The Letdown begins.</td></tr><tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>4 ct</strong></td><td>17.0 × 8.5</td><td>10.2</td><td>113.4 / 81.7 (+39%)</td><td>Big length advantage. Big width gap too.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>5 ct</strong></td><td>18.0 × 9.0</td><td>11.0</td><td>127.2 / 95.0 (+34%)</td><td>Red-carpet length. Narrow by comparison.</td></tr></tbody></table>

**Verdict:** Marquise beats round on face-up area at every carat tier on the chart. The practical ceiling for the illusion is 2ct — after that, width stops impressing. If area is the only thing that matters, marquise wins forever. If finger coverage side-to-side matters, round wins above 2.5ct.

### Finger Coverage — The Metric Nobody Puts on a Chart

Finger coverage is the percentage of your finger width the stone spans. It is the single most-asked-about measurement from buyers who have never worn either shape.

For a size-6 finger (16.5 mm wide):
- A 1ct round covers 39% (6.5 ÷ 16.5)
- A 1ct marquise covers 30% side-to-side (5 ÷ 16.5) but 60% lengthwise (10 ÷ 16.5)
- A 3ct round covers 56% (9.3 ÷ 16.5)
- A 3ct marquise covers 45% side-to-side (7.5 ÷ 16.5) but 91% lengthwise (15 ÷ 16.5)

The marquise beats the round on lengthwise coverage every time. The round beats the marquise on side-to-side coverage every time. Which one matters depends on how your partner wears rings — straight-up solo, or stacked with wedding bands.

> **"Clients always ask me which looks bigger. I say — look at your hand right now. If you want length pointing to your knuckle, buy marquise. If you want width pulling your eye across, buy round. Both can look 'big'. The direction is different."** — Farzana Hasan, GIA Diamond Expert

