**Verdict:** The natural marquise price curve is steep. A 1ct runs $4,393. A 2ct runs 5x that. A 3ct runs 13x that. The size illusion from the chart is the only way to justify stepping down a carat tier without feeling you downsized.

> **"When budget is tight I steer natural buyers to 1.5ct in H-VS1. You get maximum length illusion for about $9,000. That is half the money of a 2ct and the ring still looks bigger than most 1.5ct rounds."** — Farzana Hasan, GIA Diamond Expert

### Lab-Grown Marquise — IGI/GCAL, Same Chart, Different Prices

Lab-grown marquise follows the same size chart as natural. The chemistry and optics are identical. Only the price per carat changes — by 85–90%.

<table class="dc-custom-table"><thead><tr><th>Carat</th><th>Color/Clarity</th><th>Price</th><th>Face-Up (mm)</th><th>What this means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>1.0 ct</strong></td><td>IGI D-VVS2 Excellent</td><td><a href="https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/30016385?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational">$657</a></td><td>10.0 × 5.0</td><td>Same face-up as $4,393 natural. 85% cheaper.</td></tr><tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>1.0 ct</strong></td><td>IGI D-VVS1 Ideal</td><td><a href="https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29978545?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational">$958</a></td><td>10.0 × 5.0</td><td>Top-grade spec, still under $1k.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>2.0 ct</strong></td><td>IGI E-VVS2 Excellent</td><td><a href="https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/30118118?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational">$2,232</a></td><td>13.5 × 6.75</td><td>Face-up matches $25,560 natural 2ct. 91% cheaper.</td></tr><tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>2.0 ct</strong></td><td>IGI D-IF Ideal</td><td><a href="https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/27621458?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational">$4,409</a></td><td>13.5 × 6.75</td><td>Flawless, colorless, Ideal cut. Still ~$30k below natural equivalent.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>2.5 ct</strong></td><td>IGI E-VVS2 Excellent</td><td><a href="https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/30163241?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational">$3,283</a></td><td>14.5 × 7.25</td><td>$30k+ saved vs natural at the same spec.</td></tr></tbody></table>

**Verdict:** Lab-grown breaks the natural size chart math. A 2ct lab marquise costs less than a 1ct natural. The physical face-up chart is identical. If you only care about how the stone looks on the finger, lab is the obvious answer. If you care about resale, natural still rules.

> **"Nine of my ten engagement ring clients last year walked out with lab marquise. The size chart is the same. The sparkle is the same. The savings go into a better setting or a bigger size tier."** — Farzana Hasan, GIA Diamond Expert

## The Width Floor — Why 3ct Marquise Disappoints Buyers

**The Width Floor is the physical reason the Elongation Illusion stops working above 2.5 ct.** Marquise width is roughly half the length. At 3ct the length is 15 mm, so the width is 7.5 mm — the same width as a 1.75ct round.

Every carat tier above 2.5 ct keeps adding length but barely adds width. A 5ct marquise is 18 mm long and 9 mm wide. The width is still less than a 3ct round. The length just gets more dramatic.

Buyers see "3 carat" on the certificate and expect dominance. The eye registers width as the primary "size" cue when looking at a ring from across a table. Length registers as "slim" past a certain ratio. The Width Floor is why the chart feels dishonest at 3ct and above — the carat number doesn't translate to width expansion.

### How to Beat the Width Floor

Three ways to beat the Width Floor if you still want 3ct+ in marquise:

**Option 1 — Drop the L:W ratio to 1.75.** A 3ct at 1.75 L:W is 14.0 × 8.0 mm. You lose 1 mm of length but gain 0.5 mm of width. Looks "wider" without changing carat.

**Option 2 — Add a halo.** A pavé halo adds 1.5–2 mm of visual width around the stone. A 3ct marquise with halo reads as 10 mm wide to the eye. Full options in our [marquise halo engagement ring guide](/marquise-halo-engagement-ring).

**Option 3 — Buy east-west.** East-west mounting rotates the marquise 90 degrees so the length runs across the finger, not along it. The "width" problem disappears because length becomes the finger-coverage axis. See [east-west marquise diamond ring](/east-west-marquise-diamond-ring) for setting examples.

## Marquise Diamond Size Chart by Finger Size

Size perception depends on the finger as much as the stone. Below is the marquise diamond size chart cross-referenced to common ring sizes.

<table class="dc-custom-table"><thead><tr><th>Ring Size</th><th>Finger Width (mm)</th><th>Looks "Big" At</th><th>Looks "Overpowering" At</th><th>What this means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Size 4</strong></td><td>14.5</td><td>0.75ct marquise</td><td>2.5ct+</td><td>Petite fingers. Even 1ct marquise reads as substantial.</td></tr><tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>Size 5</strong></td><td>15.5</td><td>1ct marquise</td><td>3ct+</td><td>The sweet spot for 1–1.5ct. Any bigger starts covering knuckles.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Size 6</strong></td><td>16.5</td><td>1.25ct marquise</td><td>3.5ct+</td><td>Most common size. Chart hits normal here.</td></tr><tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>Size 7</strong></td><td>17.3</td><td>1.5ct marquise</td><td>4ct+</td><td>Needs more length to register as "big ring." Buy 1.5–2ct.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Size 8</strong></td><td>18.2</td><td>2ct marquise</td><td>5ct+</td><td>Full-size finger. 1ct marquise reads as delicate. Scale up.</td></tr><tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>Size 9</strong></td><td>19.0</td><td>2.5ct marquise</td><td>5.5ct+</td><td>Longer fingers absorb larger stones well. Length looks balanced.</td></tr></tbody></table>

**Verdict:** The Width Floor matters more on smaller fingers. On a size 4 or 5, even a 3ct marquise's narrow width still covers decent finger area. On a size 8+, you need to scale carat up fast or the stone looks lost. Buy to the finger, not to a generic "1ct is standard" rule.

## Optimization Matrix — Which Marquise Carat for Which Buyer

<table class="dc-custom-table"><thead><tr><th>Buyer Priority</th><th>Best Carat</th><th>Why</th><th>What this means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Budget under $4,000</strong></td><td>0.75ct natural or 1.5ct lab</td><td>Both read as 1ct+ face-up. Lab wins on size per dollar.</td><td>Chart gets you to "1 carat appearance" for under $1k with lab.</td></tr><tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>Budget $4,000–8,000</strong></td><td>1ct natural or 2ct lab</td><td>Natural at 1ct is the honest-size sweet spot. Lab 2ct beats it on face-up.</td><td>Pick natural for resale, lab for size.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Budget $8,000–15,000</strong></td><td>1.5ct natural</td><td>The Peak. Biggest face-up illusion in the chart.</td><td>Buy here if you want maximum "ring look" for a mid-range budget.</td></tr><tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>Budget $15,000–30,000</strong></td><td>2ct natural</td><td>Length starts dominating. Still inside the illusion.</td><td>Last carat tier where you get "bigger than expected" perception.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Budget $30,000+</strong></td><td>2.5ct natural + halo</td><td>Avoids the Width Floor at 3ct. Halo gives perceived width.</td><td>Smarter than jumping to 3ct bare. Same visual size, 40% less money.</td></tr><tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>Red-carpet statement</strong></td><td>4ct+ natural or 5ct lab</td><td>Length is the whole point. Width secondary.</td><td>Chart agrees: this is where marquise's shape purpose actually pays.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Petite finger (size 4–5)</strong></td><td>1ct marquise, L:W 2.00</td><td>Length flatters narrow finger. Width stays in-scale.</td><td>Classic ratio. Photographs well on small hands.</td></tr><tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>Larger finger (size 7+)</strong></td><td>1.5ct–2ct, L:W 1.75</td><td>Shorter ratio adds width to match finger scale.</td><td>Don't buy a narrow 2.00 L:W on a size 8 — looks skinny.</td></tr></tbody></table>

**Verdict:** The chart is a tool, not a prescription. Match the carat tier to the budget first, then to the finger size, then to the shape goal. Buyers who try to solve all three at once end up overspending. One priority per decision.

## Frequently Asked Questions

### How big is a 1 carat marquise diamond?

A 1 carat marquise diamond is **10.0 × 5.0 mm** at the classic 2.00 length-to-width ratio. Face-up area is 39.3 mm², which is 18% larger than a 1 carat round diamond (33.2 mm²). On most fingers, a 1ct marquise reads as 1.25ct round in apparent size.

### How does a marquise diamond size chart compare to round?

A marquise diamond size chart beats round on face-up area at every carat weight. The gap peaks at 1.5ct (31% larger face-up than 1.5ct round) and stays positive through 5ct. Width is where marquise loses — above 2.5ct, a round of the same carat is wider.

### What marquise carat looks biggest for the price?

**1.5 carat marquise** gives the biggest face-up size per dollar on the chart. At 12.0 × 6.0 mm, it reads bigger than a 2ct round while costing 30% less. We call this The Peak — the point where length illusion maxes out before the Width Floor starts to penalize larger carat weights.

### Does a 2 carat marquise look bigger than a 2 carat round?

Yes, significantly. A 2 carat marquise is 13.5 × 6.75 mm; a 2 carat round is 8.2 mm across. The marquise face-up area is 71.6 mm² against the round's 52.8 mm² — a 36% advantage. Length-wise, the marquise is 65% longer.

### Is a 3 carat marquise worth it?

Depends what you want. A 3 carat marquise is 15.0 × 7.5 mm — long, elegant, dramatic. But the width is only 7.5 mm, narrower than a 1.75 carat round. If you want "huge and wide," buy round. If you want "long and striking," 3 carat marquise delivers.

### What is the ideal length-to-width ratio for a marquise?

**1.75 to 2.10** is the standard range. A 2.00 L:W is the classic marquise silhouette and the one most size charts use. Narrower than 1.75 looks square-ish and loses the elegant point; wider than 2.10 looks like a sliver and amplifies bow-tie. Full detail in our [marquise L:W ratio guide](/marquise-diamond-length-to-width-ratio).

### How many mm is a 0.5 carat marquise?

A 0.5 carat marquise measures **8.0 × 4.0 mm** at a 2.00 L:W ratio. The face-up area (25.1 mm²) is 21% larger than a 0.5ct round (20.8 mm²). This is the smallest size that reads as an engagement-ring stone rather than an accent.

### Does marquise look bigger than oval at the same carat?

Barely. A 1ct oval is roughly 8.0 × 6.0 mm, so 37.7 mm² face-up. A 1ct marquise is 10.0 × 5.0 mm, so 39.3 mm². Marquise wins on length but loses on width. Which "looks bigger" depends on viewing angle — see our [oval vs marquise diamond](/oval-vs-marquise-diamond) comparison.

### Will a marquise bow-tie make it look smaller?

Yes. A severe bow-tie — the dark zone across the middle of a marquise — can shrink apparent face-up size by 20–30% because the dark area reads as "missing stone." A well-cut marquise has a faint bow-tie that reads as shading, not a hole. See [marquise diamond bow-tie](/marquise-diamond-bow-tie) for how to spot one.

### What size marquise is best for a size 7 finger?

**1.5ct to 2ct** marquise fits a size 7 finger well. At 12.0–13.5 mm long, the stone covers 72–78% of the finger lengthwise without crossing the knuckle. Smaller than 1.5ct reads as delicate on a size 7; larger than 2ct starts to overpower.

### How wide is a 5 carat marquise diamond?

A 5 carat marquise is **9.0 mm wide**. That is the same width as a 3 carat round (9.3 mm). The length is where 5ct wins — 18 mm, which crosses the knuckle on most fingers. If width is what you want, 5ct marquise is not it.

### Is marquise the biggest-looking cut per carat?

Marquise ties with pear for the largest face-up area per carat among popular shapes. Oval is close behind. Round is the smallest face-up per carat because the depth percentage runs higher (62% vs marquise's ~60%). Full ranking in our [diamond shapes guide](/diamond-shapes-guide).

### How much is a 1 carat marquise diamond?

A 1 carat natural marquise diamond starts around **$4,049** for I-VS2 Ideal Cut in current certified market inventory. Our pick is the [1ct H-VS2 Ideal at $4,393](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/30259333?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational). Lab-grown 1ct marquise starts at $629.

## Final Verdict — The Best Marquise Carat for 4 Buyer Scenarios

### Scenario 1 — First Engagement Ring, Budget $5,000

**Buy a 1ct natural marquise in H-VS2 Ideal Cut.** Our pick is the [H-VS2 Ideal at $4,393](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/30259333?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational). Pair with a simple [solitaire setting](/marquise-solitaire-engagement-ring) in 14k white gold. Rings in at $5,500 total. Reads as 1.25ct round on the finger.

### Scenario 2 — Biggest-Looking Ring Per Dollar

**Buy a 1.5ct natural marquise in G-VS2.** This is The Peak. At 12 × 6 mm face-up, it beats a 2ct round on area and runs 30% less money. Budget $9,000–12,000 for the stone, $1,000–2,000 for a halo setting that adds another 2 mm of perceived width.

### Scenario 3 — Red-Carpet Statement, Budget $50,000+

**Buy a 2.5ct–3ct natural marquise with a pavé halo.** Our pick at 3ct is the [F-VS2 Ideal at $58,575](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29142250?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational). The halo cancels the Width Floor. The length still delivers maximum drama. Skip bare 3ct — the halo is non-negotiable at this size.

### Scenario 4 — Maximum Size, Minimum Budget

**Buy a 2ct lab-grown marquise in D-IF Ideal Cut.** The [D-IF Ideal at $4,409](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/27621458?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) is the single best size-per-dollar stone on the chart. 13.5 × 6.75 mm face-up for the price of a 1ct natural. Pair with a 14k white gold solitaire for $5,500 all-in.

## How to Verify Your Marquise Matches the Size Chart

A marquise diamond size chart is only useful if the actual stone matches the chart. Blue Nile, James Allen, and every certified retailer publish measurements on the certificate. You have to read them before you buy.

### The 3 Measurements That Must Match

**Measurement 1 — Length.** Printed on the GIA or IGI certificate as the first number in the "Measurements" field. Example: a 1ct marquise shows "10.00 × 5.00 × 3.00 mm." The first number is length.

**Measurement 2 — Width.** Second number in the same field. For a 1ct marquise at 2.00 L:W, width should land at 5.00 mm. Narrower = thinner silhouette. Wider = closer to oval shape.

**Measurement 3 — Depth.** Third number. For marquise the depth percentage sits best at 59–63%. Depth above 63% means the stone carries weight below the girdle you can't see from the top. Full detail in the [marquise ideal proportions guide](/marquise-cut-diamond-ideal-proportions).

### The Depth Trap — Carat Weight That Hides Below the Girdle

A 1ct marquise with depth of 68% weighs the same as a 1ct marquise with depth of 60%. The face-up size is not the same. The deeper stone hides 15–20% of its carat weight in the pavilion, below where the eye can see.

I verify every stone against the chart measurements before I quote a client. If a listed 1ct marquise measures 9.6 × 5.0 mm instead of 10.0 × 5.0 mm, that's a deep-cut stone wasting 400 µm of face-up length. The certificate reads "1.00 ct" but the ring reads "0.9 ct." Chart first, carat second.

### Why Blue Nile's HD Video Beats the Measurement Field

Measurements don't show bow-tie. They don't show extinction zones. They don't show cutter asymmetry. Blue Nile and James Allen both publish 360° HD videos for every certified diamond — use them.

When I vet a stone for a client, I always open the HD video alongside the chart measurements. I check for bow-tie first (dark zone across the centre), symmetry second (does one half match the other), and sparkle distribution third (even light across the full stone, or dead patches). None of this is on the certificate.

